  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Tuesday/...Going into Saturday\nmorning, the strong high pressure still looks to keep the front \nfurther westward, not fully pushing into the rest of the panhandle\nuntil Saturday evening into Saturday night. The timing of this \nhas increased in confidence, however remains somewhat uncertain \nfor more inland parts of the panhandle from Juneau southward on if\nthe precipitation onset will be more in the evening or pushed \nlater into the overnight hours, largely dependent on how quickly \nthe high pressure begins to break down and allow for the system to\npush in further than the outer coast. Model agreement is however \nnarrowing in on the timing of this first front pushing east across\nthe panhandle Saturday evening through Sunday morning, with the \nhighest rainfall totals expected along the NE Gulf coastline \naround Yakutat. Generally expecting around 2 to 3 inches in 24 \nhours for the Yakutat area Friday night through Saturday night.\n\nThe precipitation type expected is still a bit uncertain for the \nSaturday night period and will depend largely on the location, as \nsome parts of the northern panhandle further inland from the outer\nGulf coast may see cold air damming near the surface Saturday \nnight into the overnight hours as the front initially moves in. \nWith this in place, some locations at sea level in the northern \npanhandle may initially see rain with snow mixing in before \nquickly transitioning to rain. Any snow mixing in is low in \nconfidence at this time still, and will not have any expected \naccumulation at sea level.\n\nThis more active pattern will continue into the start of next \nweek as a series of lows move into the Gulf, keeping wetter and \ncomparatively warmer weather in the forecast.",
